<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-7-11-capstone" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Putting It Together: Capstone Case Study</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      You now have the tools you need to solve a problem that requires storing, manipulating, and accessing a collection of data. Let’s put it all together with a program that reads records of object data from a file, sorts the records, and produces a map overlay that can be rendered in a browser.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-7-11-1-problem">
    <title>The Problem</title>

    <p>
      Imagine you are given a map of trails and landmarks along with a text file of the names and <c>(x, y)</c> coordinates for the sites. You’re asked to write a program that will read the data in, alphabetize it by name, and present the list of sites to the user. The user will choose a site, and your program will produce an HTML document that displays the map with the user’s site choice highlighted.
    </p>

    <image source="figures/boston_map.png" width="90%">
      <shortdescription>Park map of Boston National Historical Park showing the Freedom Trail and Black Heritage Trail.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          Official National Park Service map of Boston National Historical Park and surrounding neighborhoods, including Cambridge, Beacon Hill, Downtown Boston, the North End, and Charlestown. The red Freedom Trail line and blue Black Heritage Trail line are drawn across the street grid with landmark labels.
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
    <p>
      Park map of Boston National Historic Park retrieved from <url href="https://npgallery.nps.gov/HFC/Cartography" />.
    </p>

    <p>
      A record of site information is organized as the site name on the first line (text), the number of points making up the site (integer), and then the <c>x, y</c> coordinate for each point, each pair on its own line. For example:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[African Meeting House
1
470 690]]></program>

    <p>
      This site is a landmark and is therefore represented by only one coordinate. The output HTML is boilerplate, except for three insertions that your program will make from the chosen site. The name as the title and heading of the page:
    </p>

    <program language="html"><![CDATA[<title>African Meeting House</title>
<h1>African Meeting House</h1>]]></program>

    <p>
      And the list of points to either highlight with a red circle (for a landmark) or a yellow line (for a trail):
    </p>

    <program language="javascript"><![CDATA[const points = [
{x: 470.0, y: 690.0}
];]]></program>

    <p>
      When the HTML page is rendered in a browser, the user will see this (this image is slightly cropped), with “African Meeting House” as the title of the tab:
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-7-24-african-meeting-house">
      <caption>Tourism map of Boston with the African Meeting House marked with a red dot surrounded by a red circle.</caption>
      <image source="figures/african_meeting_house.png" width="85%">
        <shortdescription>Browser rendering of the Boston map with the African Meeting House highlighted by a red dot and circle.</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A slightly cropped browser view displaying the heading "African Meeting House" at the top above the Boston National Historical Park map. In Beacon Hill, the African Meeting House location is highlighted with a solid red dot surrounded by a red circle.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A trail record consists of a name and the number of points, followed by that number of lines of point data:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[Black Heritage Trail
17
526 780
525 772
